<p>Picture a scale-up that takes an open-weight model, continues pre-training it on a large domain corpus, fine-tunes it further and offers it to customers across Europe. Is it a deployer of someone else's model, or the provider of a new general-purpose AI model? Under the EU AI Act the answer changes the paperwork considerably, and it now carries enforcement risk.</p>
<p>This is an architect's reading of public guidance and law firm commentary, not legal advice.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where things stand</h2>
<p>The AI Act's obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models have applied since 2 August 2025. The Commission said the AI Office was preparing its compliance assessments ahead of enforcement of those rules from August 2026<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. A Cloud Security Alliance research note published on 3 August 2026 summarised the AI Office's powers as including requests for documentation, model evaluations, corrective measures and fines<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Alongside the law sit two practical instruments. The <strong>GPAI Code of Practice</strong>, published on 10 July 2025, has three chapters: transparency, copyright, and safety and security. The first two give all GPAI providers a way to demonstrate compliance with Article 53; the third applies only to providers of models with systemic risk under Article 55<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. The transparency chapter includes a Model Documentation Form<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The Commission also finalised a template for the public summary of training data<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The line between deployer and provider</h2>
<p>The Commission's guidelines on GPAI providers address downstream modification directly. As summarised by commentators<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>:</p>
<ul>
<li>An indicative criterion is whether the <strong>training compute used for the modification exceeds one third</strong> of the compute used to train the original model.</li>
<li>If the original compute isn't known, one third of the threshold for presuming a model is general-purpose is used instead.</li>
<li><strong>Minor changes</strong> typically don't make you a provider.</li>
<li>If you do become a provider, your obligations generally concern <strong>the modification</strong>: documentation, training data summary and copyright policy covering the additional compute and data. Modifying a model with systemic risk brings the full systemic-risk obligations.</li>
</ul>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>What you do</th><th>Likely position</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Prompting, RAG, tool use, agents on a hosted model</td><td>Not modifying the model</td></tr>
<tr><td>Light fine-tuning or adapters on modest data</td><td>Typically a minor change</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large continued pre-training or extensive fine-tuning</td><td>Check the compute ratio carefully</td></tr>
<tr><td>Modifying a model already classed as systemic-risk</td><td>Get specific legal advice early</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><p>Most teams are nowhere near the line. The ones that are usually know it, because they are spending serious money on GPUs. That spend should come with a compliance budget.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why this matters even if you are well below it</h2>
<p>First, customers ask. In enterprise procurement, "we use RAG, not fine-tuning" is a much easier answer with evidence behind it. Second, upstream providers' documentation flows down to you: the Model Documentation Form exists partly so downstream providers can understand the models they build on<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Third, the same records support obligations that apply to your AI systems regardless of the model rules, such as the literacy duty in <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-ai-literacy-article-4/">Article 4</a> and the timeline in <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-digital-omnibus-roadmap/">the Digital Omnibus roadmap</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd put in place</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Log training compute for every model you train or tune</strong>, with the method used to estimate it. It's cheap to capture at the time and very hard to reconstruct later.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a model lineage record</strong>: base model and version, upstream documentation received, datasets, licences and changes. The supply chain practices in <a href="/insights/ai-model-supply-chain-security/">AI model supply chain security</a> overlap heavily.</li>
<li><strong>Document data provenance and copyright handling</strong> for any data you train on, including how rights reservations are respected.</li>
<li><strong>Prefer adaptation over retraining</strong> where it meets the need. RAG and lighter tuning are usually cheaper as well as simpler to govern; see <a href="/insights/long-context-vs-rag/">long context vs RAG</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Map once, reuse often.</strong> Tie these records into a framework such as <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001</a> so the same evidence serves EU, US and Gulf requirements.</li>
</ol>
</section>

